\f1\fs22 Dr Janine Anstey looked up. \'93That\'92s good.\'94\
\'93I\'92m being lied to by furniture.\'94\
The resistance was set low. It had been lower three months ago, though neither of them mentioned that. Janine had been checking Pin Cushion\'92s riding form from the beginning; her feet stayed in the straps and her knees tracked well enough for the first few minutes. Then her right hip began to lift slightly on the downstroke. A little later she shifted her weight across the saddle to help the left leg through.\
Janine knew the sequence. First, \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Pin Cushion\'92s \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
thighs would tire. Then her hips would compensate. She\'92d get angry with the bike.\
\'93Don\'92t watch me,\'94 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Pin Cushion said.\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
Pin Cushion pedalled another half a minute and stopped. Getting off immediately was sometimes worse. She was never sure what her legs would do after.\
\'93Need a hand?\'94\
\'93No.\'94\
Janine waited.\
Pin Cushion put one foot on the floor, then the other. She leaned forward, planted both hands on her thighs and pushed herself upright in stages.\
Janine looked away before she was caught.\
Pin Cushion walked towards the lounge. Her gait was good for the first few steps, then acquired a slight side-to-side correction that, months ago, Janine had stopped pretending not to recognise.\
Janine had \'91renovated\'92 the stairs with a second handrail; they both knew it was mobility management. Pin Cushion objected to the handrail the same way she objected to physiotherapy, blood tests, muscle tests and therapists saying 
\f2\i well done
\f1\i0  when anyone could see things were getting done worse. Pin Cushion was deteriorating right on schedule.\
\'93Seven injections today,\'94 Pin Cushion said with the stoic acceptance only a twelve-year-old could pull off. \'93Eight if you count the one they messed up.\'94\
Janine started to apologise.\
\'93Don\'92t, Mum.\'94\
Pin Cushion\'92s self-designated nickname made medical clipboard procedure bearable.\
\'93Name?\'94 a nurse would ask, with her pen poised to tick a box.\
\'93Pin Cushion Anstey.\'94\
The nurses Pin Cushion liked would smile and cross out her given name and write 
\f2\i Pin Cushion
\f1\i0  above. Everyone at the hospital and clinics knew who she was. The nurses who corrected her name for the records never called her 
\f2\i brave
